+# Contract: `accounts` command-line interface — password reset
+
+**Feature**: `002-password-reset` | **Spec**: [../spec.md](../spec.md)
+
+This document extends [`../../001-accounts-cli/contracts/cli.md`](../../001-accounts-cli/contracts/cli.md),
+which stays in force for the five existing commands. Everything here is assertable and each
+row names the requirement it comes from; message strings are exact and complete.
+
+**One change to the existing contract**: the usage line (FR-040). Nothing else about
+`register`, `login`, `unlock`, `passwd` or `delete` changes in what it accepts, reads, prints
+or returns (FR-041) — see [Frozen behaviour](#frozen-behaviour-of-the-five-existing-commands).
+
+---
+
+## Synopsis
+
+```text
+python -m accounts --store PATH COMMAND USERNAME [--ttl SECONDS]
+```
+
+- `COMMAND` is one of `register`, `login`, `unlock`, `passwd`, `delete`, `reset-token`,
+  `reset`. (FR-031)
+- `--ttl SECONDS` is accepted by `reset-token` only. (FR-033)
+- `PATH`, `USERNAME`, the `PYTHONPATH` requirement and the Python 3.11+ / stdlib-only baseline
+  are as in the 001 contract.
+
+### Argument grammar — additions
+
+| Input | Behaviour | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| `reset-token USERNAME` | The documented form; lifetime 900 seconds. | FR-032, FR-033 |
+| `reset-token USERNAME --ttl 1` … `--ttl 86400` | Accepted; both edges are valid. | FR-033 |
+| `--ttl` before the positionals, or between them | Accepted — located by scan, like `--store`. | FR-033, D-014 |
+| `--ttl=900` | **Usage error.** Only the space-separated form is supported. | FR-033, D-014 |
+| `--ttl` with no following value | Usage error. | FR-033 |
+| `--ttl abc`, `--ttl ""`, `--ttl " 5"`, `--ttl +5`, `--ttl 1_0`, `--ttl 1.5`, `--ttl 0x10`, `--ttl ²`, `--ttl ١٢` | Usage error — only a non-empty run of ASCII digits `0`–`9` is a number here. | FR-033, D-014 |
+| `--ttl 0`, `--ttl -5` | Usage error — the minimum is 1. | FR-033 |
+| `--ttl 86401`, `--ttl 99999999999999999999` | Usage error — refused, **not** clamped. | FR-033 |
+| `--ttl` given twice | Usage error. | D-014 |
+| `--ttl` with `register`, `login`, `unlock`, `passwd`, `delete`, or `reset` | Usage error. | FR-033 |
+| `reset` with a third positional, or any other flag | Usage error, as for every command. | FR-040 |
+
+No token is ever an argument: `reset` reads it from standard input, exactly as passwords are
+read. (FR-035, FR-044)
+
+### Usage error
+
+```text
+usage: python -m accounts --store PATH {register|login|unlock|passwd|delete|reset-token|reset} USERNAME [--ttl SECONDS]
+```
+
+on **stderr**, exit **2**, and no store file created or modified. This exact string replaces
+the 001 usage line for every malformed invocation of every command — the one and only visible
+change to an existing command. (FR-040)
+
+---
+
+## Standard input protocol — additions
+
+| Command | Lines read, in order | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| `reset-token` | **0** — reads nothing at all | FR-034 |
+| `reset` | **2** — the token, then the new password | FR-035 |
+
+The 001 line rules apply unchanged to both of `reset`'s lines: content up to and excluding the
+line break, nothing stripped, leading and trailing whitespace significant, `\r\n` and `\n` both
+terminating, input beyond the two lines ignored. (FR-035)
+
+| Input to `reset` | Outcome | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| End of input before both lines (nothing at all, or only the token line) | `missing password input` / **2**, store unchanged — judged **before** the account is looked up | FR-036, FR-037 |
+| An empty token line | A token of length 0, which is not the issued token → `invalid token` / **1** (not missing input) | FR-035, edge cases |
+| An empty new-password line with a valid token | A password of length 0 → `password too short` / **2**, token still usable | FR-036a, FR-036f |
+| A token line with leading or trailing spaces | Taken verbatim → not the issued token → `invalid token` / **1** | FR-035 |
+| A third and further line | Ignored, not an error | FR-035 |
+
+---
+
+## Exit statuses
+
+Unchanged vocabulary and meanings (FR-043). What the new commands can produce:
+
+| Status | Meaning | `reset-token` | `reset` |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 0 | Success | yes | yes |
+| 1 | Invalid credentials — here, a refused token | no | yes |
+| 2 | Invalid input — usage, missing input, or a too-short new password | yes (usage only) | yes |
+| 3 | Account does not exist | yes | yes |
+| 4 | Account locked | **never** | **never** |
+| 5 | Store unusable | yes | yes |
+
+Status **4 is not produced by either new command**: both act on a locked account (FR-038,
+FR-043).
+
+---
+
+## Messages — additions
+
+| Message | Stream | Status | Produced by | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| *(the token, 43 characters, alone on its line)* | stdout | 0 | `reset-token` success | FR-032, FR-034a |
+| `password reset` | stdout | 0 | `reset` success | FR-042 |
+| `invalid token` | stderr | 1 | `reset` with a token that is wrong, already used, expired, cancelled, or never issued | FR-042 |
+| `no such account` | stderr | 3 | `reset-token` / `reset` on an unknown account — reused verbatim | FR-042 |
+| `password too short` | stderr | 2 | `reset` with a new password under 12 characters — reused verbatim | FR-042 |
+| `missing password input` | stderr | 2 | `reset` when input ends before both lines — reused verbatim | FR-042 |
+| `usage: … {register\|login\|unlock\|passwd\|delete\|reset-token\|reset} USERNAME [--ttl SECONDS]` | stderr | 2 | malformed arguments, any command | FR-040 |
+| `store unusable: <cause>` | stderr | 5 | unreadable or corrupt store, including malformed token state | FR-037, FR-039 |
+
+No new message is introduced beyond `password reset` and `invalid token` (FR-042). On success
+both commands write **nothing to stderr**; on failure both write to **stderr only** and nothing
+to stdout (FR-042).
+
+`reset-token`'s successful output is exactly one line containing exactly the token — no label,
+no expiry, no blank line, nothing on stderr (FR-032, clarified 2026-11-11).
+
+---
+
+## Per-command behaviour
+
+Global order for every command, unchanged: argument parsing (2) → store load (5) → read the
+required input lines (2) → the command's own checks. (FR-037)
+
+### `reset-token USERNAME [--ttl SECONDS]`
+
+1. Reads no input. (FR-034)
+2. No such account → `no such account`, stderr, **3**, nothing on stdout, no store file
+   created or modified. (FR-032, FR-042)
+3. Otherwise → create a token, record it in the store, print the token alone on stdout, exit
+   **0**. Succeeds whether or not the account is locked. (FR-032)
+
+Guarantees on success:
+
+- The password material, `failed_logins` and `locked` are **not** touched — a locked account
+  stays locked, and `login` with the correct password still reports `account locked` / 4 while
+  the token is merely outstanding. (FR-032, FR-038)
+- Any token already outstanding for that account is **replaced**; the old token is invalid from
+  that moment. Exactly one token is valid per account. (FR-032)
+- Two invocations never print the same token. (FR-034a)
+- No password derivation is performed. (SC-018)
+- The token is not recoverable from the store afterwards, and is printed by nothing else, ever.
+  (FR-034b, FR-044)
+
+### `reset USERNAME`
+
+Reads two lines: the token, then the new password.
+
+1. No such account → `no such account`, stderr, **3**, store unchanged. (FR-036a)
+2. The presented token is not the account's outstanding token, or there is none, or it has
+   already been used, or it has been cancelled by a successful `passwd`, or its lifetime has
+   elapsed → `invalid token`, stderr, **1**, store unchanged — the outstanding token, the
+   password, the tally and the lock state are all exactly as they were. (FR-036a, FR-036d,
+   FR-036e, FR-038a)
+3. The new password is under 12 characters → `password too short`, stderr, **2**, store
+   unchanged, and **the token remains usable**. (FR-036a, FR-036f)
+4. Otherwise → replace the password material with freshly derived material, remove the token,
+   clear `locked`, reset `failed_logins` to 0, print `password reset` on stdout, exit **0**.
+   (FR-036b)
+
+Order is assertable: a **wrong token together with a too-short password exits 1**, because the
+token is judged before the password's length. (FR-036a, acceptance 3.3)
+
+After a successful `reset`:
+
+| Property | Guarantee | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| New password | `login` with it prints `ok` / 0 | FR-036b |
+| Previous password | `login` with it prints `invalid credentials` / 1 | FR-036b |
+| Lock | cleared — `login` succeeds with no `unlock`, whether or not the account was locked before | FR-036b |
+| Tally | 0 | FR-036b |
+| The spent token | any later non-overlapping `reset` with it → `invalid token` / 1 | FR-036c |
+| Derivations | exactly one, performed only after the token was accepted | SC-018 |
+
+---
+
+## Token lifecycle, as assertable rows
+
+Each row is "after X, `reset` with the token T issued earlier yields …". (SC-020)
+
+| Event after the token was issued | `reset` with that token |
+|---|---|
+| nothing | **succeeds** (0) |
+| a successful `reset` with it | `invalid token` / 1 (FR-036c) |
+| a later `reset-token` for the same account | `invalid token` / 1 — only the newest works (FR-032) |
+| a successful `passwd` | `invalid token` / 1 (FR-038a) |
+| a `delete` (even if the username is registered again) | `invalid token` / 1 (FR-036g) |
+| its lifetime elapsed | `invalid token` / 1 (FR-036d) |
+| a `reset` refused with `invalid token` | **succeeds** (0) — a failed attempt does not disarm it (FR-036e) |
+| a `reset` refused with `password too short` | **succeeds** (0) (FR-036f) |
+| a **failed** `passwd` (wrong current password, or short new password) | **succeeds** (0) (FR-038a) |
+| a **failed** `delete` (wrong password) | **succeeds** (0) (FR-038) |
+| a `register`, `login` (success or failure, including a lockout), or `unlock` | **succeeds** (0) (FR-038) |
+
+---
+
+## Frozen behaviour of the five existing commands
+
+Asserted by the shipped suite, which continues to pass (SC-010):
+
+| Command | Change |
+|---|---|
+| `register` | none, except the usage line for a malformed invocation |
+| `login` | none |
+| `unlock` | none |
+| `passwd` | **store effect only**: a successful `passwd` also removes the account's `reset_token`. Arguments, input lines, messages, streams, statuses, and its effect on the password, the tally and the lock state are all unchanged. A failed `passwd` changes nothing. (FR-038a) |
+| `delete` | **store effect only**: the token goes with the record, which is automatic. No other change. (FR-036g) |
+
+The usage line of FR-040 is the only change visible in any existing command's output or
+status. (FR-040, FR-041)
+
+---
+
+## Store-related behaviour
+
+| Condition | Behaviour | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| Store file does not exist | The empty store → both new commands report `no such account` / 3 and create no file | FR-002, FR-037 |
+| Store unreadable, corrupt, or holding malformed token state | `store unusable: <cause>` / 5 for both new commands — never treated as empty, and judged before the account lookup | FR-037, FR-039 |
+| Store written before this feature existed | Loads and works for all seven commands, no migration, same format marker | FR-039, SC-019 |
+| Store holding token state | Read and rewritten by all seven commands; the token state of accounts a command does not alter is preserved | FR-039, SC-019 |
+| Any successful write | The inherited whole-file atomic replace at mode `0o600` | FR-029b, FR-029c, FR-038b |
+| Any failing command | Store left exactly as it was (the `login` tally increment remains the one exception) | FR-028, FR-038 |
+
+Overlapping invocations against one store stay out of scope, as for the five existing commands:
+no inter-process locking is provided, one of two overlapping runs may lose its change, but no
+interleaving can corrupt the store or revive a token whose `reset` has been committed.
+(FR-038b)
+
+See [store-file.md](./store-file.md) for the encoding of token state.
+
+---
+
+## Performance contract
+
+- Each new command completes within **1 second** for a store of up to 1,000 accounts; the full
+  suite still within 60 seconds. (SC-018)
+- `reset-token` performs **no** password derivation; `reset` performs **exactly one**, and only
+  after the token has been accepted. Token fingerprinting is a single SHA-256 (microseconds).
+  (SC-018, [../research.md](../research.md) D-012)
+
+---
+
+## Internal entry point
+
+Unchanged in signature and discipline:
+
+```python
+accounts.cli.main(argv: list[str], stdin: TextIO, stdout: TextIO, stderr: TextIO) -> int
+```
+
+`--ttl` is parsed inside `main` from `argv`; nothing new reads an interpreter global, an
+environment variable or a configuration file, and nothing writes outside the store path given
+by `--store`. (FR-044, constitution: No hidden I/O)
