+# Contract: store file format — reset-token state
+
+**Feature**: `002-password-reset` | **Spec**: [../spec.md](../spec.md) | **Requirements**: FR-034b, FR-036d, FR-039
+
+This document extends [`../../001-accounts-cli/contracts/store-file.md`](../../001-accounts-cli/contracts/store-file.md),
+which remains canonical for everything it covers. The encoding, the format marker, the top-level
+shape, the six required account fields, the write protocol and the existing corruption
+conditions are all **unchanged**. One optional key is added inside the account record.
+
+Meaning of the new fields is in [../data-model.md](../data-model.md); this document fixes the
+encoding.
+
+---
+
+## What does not change (FR-039)
+
+- The format marker stays **`"accounts-store-v1"`**. No new marker, no version bump, no
+  migration step. A store written before this feature is a valid store now, and loads with no
+  token state.
+- The top level stays exactly `format` and `accounts`. Token state is **not** a top-level key.
+- The six required account fields and their constraints are untouched.
+- The write protocol is untouched: whole-document atomic replace via a same-directory temporary
+  file at mode `0o600` (FR-029b, FR-029c). Token state is written by the same single `save()`
+  that writes everything else, so a token is never committed separately from the password
+  change that spends it (FR-038b).
+- Key order and whitespace remain outside the contract — compare parsed JSON, not bytes. The
+  exception now has two members: SC-005 (no plaintext password in the bytes) and **SC-014** (no
+  token in the bytes, in any plain, base64 or hexadecimal form).
+
+## Shape with token state
+
+```json
+{
+  "format": "accounts-store-v1",
+  "accounts": {
+    "alice": {
+      "algorithm": "pbkdf2_hmac_sha256",
+      "iterations": 600000,
+      "salt": "3Q2+7wAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA==",
+      "key": "f6bX4m3kZ0F0bkYyR2hXb1lkV2hUZ0kyZ0kyZ0kyZ0k=",
+      "failed_logins": 0,
+      "locked": false,
+      "reset_token": {
+        "token_hash": "2cf24dba5fb0a30e26e83b2ac5b9e29e1b161e5c1fa7425e73043362938b9824",
+        "issued_at": 1794427356.867,
+        "ttl_seconds": 900
+      }
+    },
+    "bob": {
+      "algorithm": "pbkdf2_hmac_sha256",
+      "iterations": 600000,
+      "salt": "AAAA7wAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA==",
+      "key": "Z0kyZ0kyZ0kyZ0k0bkYyR2hXb1lkV2hUZ0kyZ0kyZ0k=",
+      "failed_logins": 0,
+      "locked": false
+    }
+  }
+}
+```
+
+`alice` has an outstanding token; `bob` has none and therefore carries **no** `reset_token`
+key — this is the normal state of an account and exactly what a record looked like before this
+feature existed. (A-7, FR-039)
+
+## Field reference — `reset_token`
+
+### Inside the account record
+
+| Key | JSON type | Required | Constraint |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `reset_token` | object | **no** | Present ⇔ a token is outstanding. Absent is the normal state. `null` is **not** "absent" — it is corruption. |
+
+### Inside the `reset_token` object
+
+| Key | JSON type | Required | Constraint |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `token_hash` | string | yes | Exactly **64** characters, each in `0123456789abcdef` — `hashlib.sha256(token.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()`. Uppercase hex is corruption, because nothing this tool writes produces it. |
+| `issued_at` | number | yes | Epoch seconds as returned by `time.time()`. Finite; must not be a bool. An integer is accepted (JSON does not distinguish), a float is what this version writes. |
+| `ttl_seconds` | integer | yes | **1 ≤ n ≤ 86400**. Must not be a bool, a float, or a numeric string. |
+
+All three are required when the key is present. Unknown **extra** keys inside the object are
+not an error and are preserved on rewrite, matching how account records already treat extras; a
+**missing** required key is corruption. (S-6 precedent, FR-039)
+
+The token itself appears nowhere in the file. (FR-034b)
+
+## Validation
+
+To decide whether a presented token `t` is the account's valid outstanding token:
+
+1. The record has a `reset_token` key — otherwise refuse.
+2. `hmac.compare_digest(hashlib.sha256(t.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest(), reset_token["token_hash"])`
+   — constant-time; a mismatch refuses. (FR-034b)
+3. `time.time() < reset_token["issued_at"] + reset_token["ttl_seconds"]` — otherwise refuse.
+   The boundary is **exclusive**: at exactly `issued_at + ttl_seconds` the token is expired.
+   (FR-036d)
+
+All three must hold. Any refusal is `invalid token` on stderr with exit 1, indistinguishable
+in output and status between "wrong", "never issued", "already used", "cancelled" and
+"expired". (FR-036a, FR-042)
+
+New token material (`reset-token`) is `secrets.token_urlsafe(32)` — 32 CSPRNG bytes rendered
+as 43 characters of `[A-Za-z0-9_-]` — with `issued_at = time.time()` and `ttl_seconds` from
+`--ttl` or the 900-second default. (FR-034a, FR-033)
+
+## Write effects on token state, by command
+
+| Command | Effect on `reset_token` | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| `reset-token` success | set, replacing any previous value | FR-032 |
+| `reset-token` failure | none — no write at all | FR-032 |
+| `reset` success | removed, in the same write as the new password material, `failed_logins: 0` and `locked: false` | FR-036b |
+| `reset` failure (1, 2, 3 or 5) | none — no write at all | FR-036e, FR-036f, FR-038 |
+| `passwd` success | removed, in the same write as the new password material | FR-038a |
+| `passwd` failure | none | FR-038a |
+| `delete` success | gone with the record | FR-036g |
+| `delete` failure | none | FR-038 |
+| `register`, `login`, `unlock` | none, under every outcome | FR-038 |
+
+Any command that rewrites the store preserves, byte-for-byte in meaning, the token state of
+every account it does not alter. (FR-039, SC-019)
+
+## Corruption conditions — additions
+
+Each produces `store unusable: <cause>` on stderr with exit **5**, for every one of the seven
+commands, and is never silently ignored nor read as "no token" (FR-039, Principle II). The
+existing conditions from the 001 contract all still apply.
+
+- `reset_token` is present and is not a JSON object — including `null`, a string, a number, a
+  boolean, or an array.
+- `reset_token` is missing `token_hash`, `issued_at` or `ttl_seconds`.
+- `token_hash` is not a string; or is not exactly 64 characters; or contains a character
+  outside `0`–`9`/`a`–`f` (including uppercase hex).
+- `issued_at` is not a number; is a boolean; or is not finite (`NaN`, `Infinity`,
+  `-Infinity` — which `json.loads` accepts by default and this tool must therefore reject).
+- `ttl_seconds` is not an integer; is a boolean; is a float (including `900.0`); or is outside
+  1…86400.
+
+As in the 001 contract, `bool` being a subclass of `int` in Python means a boolean must be
+rejected explicitly wherever a number is required: `{"ttl_seconds": true}` and
+`{"issued_at": false}` are both corruption.
+
+## Compatibility, both directions (SC-019)
+
+| Direction | Guarantee |
+|---|---|
+| Old store → this version | A file written before this feature loads without error, with no migration and no marker change; all seven commands work on it. An account in it simply has no outstanding token, so `reset` on it yields `invalid token` / 1. |
+| Store with token state → every command | All seven commands read and rewrite it without error, and preserve the token state of accounts they do not alter. |
+
+A future format change would introduce a new marker, exactly as the 001 contract says; this
+feature deliberately does not, because an optional key inside a record needs no marker change
+and FR-039 forbids one.
